## User Accounts

Five accounts were created to satisfy the lab brief. **Passwords are not committed** — substitute your own at build time. The table below describes role and password posture only.

| Username       | OU         | Role / Privileges        | Password Posture                                  |
| -------------- | ---------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------- |
| `Administrator`| Admins     | Domain Administrator     | Strong, set at promotion                          |
| `support`      | Support    | Standard user            | Strong, locally set                               |
| `production1` | Production | Standard user, domain-joined to operator WS | **Deliberately weak** per lab requirement (see note below) |
| `production2` | Production | Standard user            | Strong, locally set                               |
| `production3` | Production | Standard user            | Strong, locally set                               |

> **Deliberately weak password note** — the lab brief required one production user to have a weak password as part of the assessable surface (so that password attacks could be demonstrated against AD). That password is **not** included in this repository. Anyone reproducing the lab should set their own value and treat it as a known finding rather than something to protect.

## Group Policy

Baseline GPOs applied in the lab:

| GPO                          | Scope             | Effect                                                      |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| Default Domain Policy        | Domain            | Password length, complexity, lockout thresholds (baseline). |
| Operator-RDP-Allow           | Operator WS OU    | Enables RDP, opens 3389 to the Ops subnet.                  |
| Operator-Internet-Deny       | Operator WS OU    | Blocks outbound 80/443 at the host firewall.                |

> The lab intentionally **disables Network Level Authentication** on the operator's RDP, since one objective is to demonstrate the difference between NLA-protected and unprotected RDP. Do not replicate this on production systems.

## Domain Discovery (Reference)

Commands used to confirm AD posture during the lab. None of these write to the domain.

```powershell
# On a domain-joined host
gpresult /r
rsop.msc
secpol.msc
netplwiz
```

```bash
# From Kali (read-only enumeration once a valid credential is held)
nxc smb 172.16.10.0/24 -u <user> -p <pass> --users --groups --shares
```

## Known Configuration Issues

These are documented as findings rather than misconfigurations to fix silently — the report should reflect what was found.

- **Administrator account lacks full domain privileges** in the as-built state. Add to `Domain Admins`, `Enterprise Admins`, and `Schema Admins` as appropriate, or document the intent if reduced privileges were chosen deliberately.
- **DNS on Kali was not initially configured**, leading to name-resolution failures during reconnaissance. Static DNS pointing at `172.16.10.10` resolves it.
